# KLIPY external GIF search — example environment configuration
#
# Boru's GIF picker uses the KLIPY provider for external GIF search.
# Copy this file to `klipy.env` (or export the variable in your shell /
# desktop session launcher) and set the value to your API key.
#
#   cp docs/klipy.env.example klipy.env
#   export KLIPY_API_KEY="$(grep KLIPY_API_KEY klipy.env | cut -d= -f2-)"
#
# Where to get a key:
#   Create one in the KLIPY Partner Panel -> API Keys:
#     https://partner.klipy.com/api-keys
#   (Testing-mode keys are limited to ~100 requests/hour; request production
#   access via the Partner Panel after testing.)
#
# Security notes:
#   * NEVER commit a real key.  This file only ships a placeholder.
#   * If KLIPY_API_KEY is unset, external GIF search is disabled gracefully;
#     the picker shows a "KLIPY is not configured" state and the rest of the
#     app is unaffected.
#   * The key is read at runtime and is never embedded in the binary, never
#     logged, never written to settings.json, and never sent to peers or chat
#     messages.
#
# Privacy notes:
#   * GIF search is optional and opt-in: KLIPY is contacted only when you use
#     the GIF picker.  Search terms are sent to the KLIPY service.
#   * Boru never sends usernames, peer IDs, room IDs, message contents,
#     contacts, or attachment metadata to KLIPY, and adds no behavioural
#     analytics.  Search queries are not logged.
#   * Remote peers can never trigger a GIF search on your device, and opening
#     a conversation never auto-loads external media.
#
# Replace the placeholder below with your real key:
KLIPY_API_KEY=your_klipy_api_key_here
